  "note": "Human-authored anchor for company sectors, mirroring archetype_map.json's role in cross-sport alignment. This is DATA, version-controlled, changed only in a commit -- so a sector number can never move because someone regrouped categories in a notebook after seeing a result. Authored only after two automatic routes were measured and rejected: Wikidata P279* rollup onto declared anchors reached 51/128 (39.8%) and missed 'airline', 'software company' and 'cryptocurrency exchange'; NAICS/ISIC/SIC/GICS are 0/128.",
  "granularity_note": "24 sectors, deliberately finer than the 14 first proposed (operator chose more granularity). Finer sectors track category EXCLUSIVITY, which is how sponsorship inventory is actually priced -- an airline and a rail operator do not compete for the same category slot. Cost is per-cell power: several sectors below carry a single company, and any test split on sector must report per-cell n rather than assuming the taxonomy grants it.",
  "disjointness_note": "A company may carry several industry values and therefore several sectors. AT&T is telecom and media. Sectors are NOT forced to a primary, because inventing a primary is inventing precision. Any per-sector athlete count is non-disjoint and must be reported beside the deduplicated union.",
